n    for (int ii = 0; ii < n - s + 1; ii += B) {\n        int i_end = std::min(ii + B, n - s + 1);\n        for (int jj = 0; jj < n - s + 1; jj += B) {\n            int j_end = std::min(jj + B, n - s + 1);\n            #pragma ivdep\n            for (int i = ii; i < i_end; ++i) {\n                const long long * __restrict__ row_i_s = &pref[(i + s) * np1];\n                const long long * __restrict__ row_i   = &pref[i * np1];\n                #pragma ivdep\n                for (int j = jj; j < j_end; ++j) {\n                    long long sum = row_i_s[j + s] - row_i[j + s]\n                                   - row_i_s[j] + row_i[j];\n                    if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\n    return 0;\n}\n\nBut note: In the second pass, we used a loop over j from 0 to n inclusive. That's fine because pref has size (n+1)*(n+1). The vertical accumulation adds previous row's value; this is correct.\n\nPotential issue: We are reading input inside the first parallel for loop using read_int(). This will cause multiple threads calling getchar_unlocked concurrently, which is not thread-safe. In A, they did it sequentially in a single-threaded manner (no OpenMP). So we must NOT parallelize the input-reading pass if we use getchar_unlocked because it's not reentrant. Instead, we should read all values first into a flat array or do sequential reading and then construct prefix sums.\n\nBetter approach: Read entire matrix sequentially using fast reader into a temporary vector<int> (or directly compute horizontal sums). Since the input is large but still O(n^2), we can read row by row in a single thread, storing values temporarily? But A's method reads and computes horizontal sum on the fly without storing full matrix. That saves memory bandwidth. However, if we want to parallelize prefix construction, we need all data available first.\n\nOption 1: Read entire matrix into flat 